DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       All  of  these interfaces are available on Linux, and are used for get‐
       ting and setting the process group ID (PGID) of a  process.   The  pre‐
       ferred,  POSIX.1‐specified  ways  of doing this are: ggeettppggrrpp(void), for
       retrieving the calling process’s PGID; and  sseettppggiidd(),  for  setting  a
       process’s PGID.

       sseettppggiidd()  sets  the  PGID of the process specified by _p_i_d to _p_g_i_d.  If
       _p_i_d is zero, then the process ID of the calling process  is  used.   If
       _p_g_i_d is zero, then the PGID of the process specified by _p_i_d is made the
       same  as  its  process ID.  If sseettppggiidd() is used to move a process from
       one process group to another (as is done by some shells  when  creating
       pipelines),  both  process groups must be part of the same session (see
       sseettssiidd(2) and ccrreeddeennttiiaallss(7)).  In this case, the _p_g_i_d specifies an ex‐
       isting process group to be joined and the session ID of that group must
       match the session ID of the joining process.

       The POSIX.1 version of ggeettppggrrpp(), which takes no arguments, returns the
       PGID of the calling process.

       ggeettppggiidd() returns the PGID of the process specified by _p_i_d.  If _p_i_d  is
       zero,  the  process ID of the calling process is used.  (Retrieving the
       PGID of a process other than the caller is rarely  necessary,  and  the
       POSIX.1 ggeettppggrrpp() is preferred for that task.)

       The  System V‐style  sseettppggrrpp(), which takes no arguments, is equivalent
       to _s_e_t_p_g_i_d_(_0_, _0_).

       The BSD‐specific sseettppggrrpp() call, which takes arguments _p_i_d and _p_g_i_d, is
       a wrapper function that calls

           setpgid(pid, pgid)

       Since glibc 2.19, the BSD‐specific sseettppggrrpp() function is no longer  ex‐
       posed  by  _<_u_n_i_s_t_d_._h_>; calls should be replaced with the sseettppggiidd() call
       shown above.

       The BSD‐specific ggeettppggrrpp() call, which takes a single _p_i_d argument,  is
       a wrapper function that calls

           getpgid(pid)

       Since  glibc 2.19, the BSD‐specific ggeettppggrrpp() function is no longer ex‐
       posed by _<_u_n_i_s_t_d_._h_>; calls should be replaced with calls to the POSIX.1
       ggeettppggrrpp() which takes no arguments (if the  intent  is  to  obtain  the
       caller’s PGID), or with the ggeettppggiidd() call shown above.

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEE
       On  success,  sseettppggiidd() and sseettppggrrpp() return zero.  On error, -1 is re‐
       turned, and _e_r_r_n_o is set to indicate the error.

       The POSIX.1 ggeettppggrrpp() always returns the PGID of the caller.

       ggeettppggiidd(), and the BSD‐specific ggeettppggrrpp() return  a  process  group  on
       success.   On  error,  -1 is returned, and _e_r_r_n_o is set to indicate the
       error.

