NNOOTTEESS
       A  child  created  via  ffoorrkk(2) inherits its parent’s process group ID.
       The PGID is preserved across an eexxeeccvvee(2).

       Each process group is a member of a session and each process is a  mem‐
       ber  of  the session of which its process group is a member.  (See ccrree‐‐
       ddeennttiiaallss(7).)

       A session can have a controlling terminal.  At any time, one (and  only
       one) of the process groups in the session can be the foreground process
       group  for  the terminal; the remaining process groups are in the back‐
       ground.  If a signal is generated from the terminal (e.g.,  typing  the
       interrupt  key  to  generate  SSIIGGIINNTT), that signal is sent to the fore‐
       ground process group.  (See tteerrmmiiooss(3) for a description of the charac‐
       ters that generate signals.)  Only the  foreground  process  group  may
       rreeaadd(2)  from  the  terminal;  if  a  background process group tries to
       rreeaadd(2) from the terminal, then the group is  sent  a  SSIIGGTTTTIINN  signal,
       which  suspends  it.   The  ttccggeettppggrrpp(3) and ttccsseettppggrrpp(3) functions are
       used to get/set the foreground process group of the controlling  termi‐
       nal.

       The  sseettppggiidd() and ggeettppggrrpp() calls are used by programs such as bbaasshh(1)
       to create process groups in order to implement shell job control.

       If the termination of a process causes a process group  to  become  or‐
       phaned,  and  if  any  member  of  the  newly orphaned process group is
       stopped, then a SSIIGGHHUUPP signal followed by a SSIIGGCCOONNTT signal will be sent
       to each process in the  newly  orphaned  process  group.   An  orphaned
       process  group  is  one  in which the parent of every member of process
       group is either itself also a member of the process group or is a  mem‐
       ber  of  a  process  group  in  a  different  session (see also ccrreeddeenn‐‐
       ttiiaallss(7)).

